# Astrolabe Clockwork

Astrolabe simulation uses closed-form analytical solutions to directly calculate every angle and position.

Modify the Astrolabe so that it behaves as a clockwork simulation. The front face is the astrolabe dial. The rear face will show the gears "in" the watch.

For the hours and minutes, build a traditional 5-spring drive train:

| Wheel | Pinion Teeth | Teeth | RPH | Arbor |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Barrel | 0 | 80 | 0.125 | Mainspring, 5 windings for ~40 hr reserve |
| Center Wheel | 10 | 80 | 1 | None; use minute/cannon pinion here only if the model includes motion work on this arbor |
| Third Wheel | 10 | 75 | 8 | None |
| Fourth Wheel | 10 | 60 | 60 | Seconds Hand |
| Escape Wheel | 6 | 15 | 600 | Pallet fork / balance impulse |
| Balance Wheel | 0 | 0 | 18,000 BPH | Balance staff |

Suggest an appropriate motion train for each of the three modes - Copernican, Keplerian, and Gallilean. Each gets their own table, indicating which drive train arbor they branch off.
Ignore a date complication at this time.

Each orbital disk should drive on its own gear and arbor. 
